\DOC LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a machine-state program-logic goal across one inlined
modular-subtraction subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a
successor state and adding the arithmetic specification of the computed result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index selecting which call site (block)
within the code is stepped over; the second is a {string list} naming the
result and the two operand variables of the subtraction, which fixes the names
used in the newly introduced assumptions. The tactic takes one goal to one
goal: the conclusion's final state argument changes from the previous state
variable to a fresh successor state variable, the {eventually} body being
otherwise unchanged. Every assumption mentioning the old state — the
program-counter reading, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — is discharged and re-added at the new state,
with the program counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the
memory component written by the subroutine. A new memory-read assumption gives
the destination buffer's contents as the result variable, and one or two purely
arithmetic assumptions are added, bounding the result and stating that it is
congruent to the difference of the two operands modulo the relevant prime,
sometimes as an implication conditional on a bound on an operand.
{
           A ?- t
   ======================================  LOCAL_SUB_TWICE4_TAC n [res; op1; op2]
           A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions restated at the
new state, plus the new memory-read assumption and the new arithmetic
assumptions, and {t'} is {t} at the new state.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of transformation occurs, on goals
of this program-logic shape.

\ENDDOC
